## Test files for all problems
Screenshots uploaded to show the code running for all problems. All input files on Blackboard have been tested.

Problem 1:

The program builds a tree by randomly generating an input as instructed. It then tests the following operations:

Node ADT: 
It stores a value and two links, left and right. A missing child is None. Node(value) creates a node with both links set to None.

BST ADT operations:
    1. AddNode(value) : Inserts value. Smaller values go to the left, larger values go to the right. 
    A value that is already in the tree is ignored. The new node is always added as a leaf.
    2. FindNode(value) : Returns True if the value is in the tree and False if it is not.
    3. DeleteNode(value) : Removes the value from the tree, if present; returns "Value not found" if not. 
    4. PrintTree() : Prints all values in sorted order (inorder traversal), one per line.

The BST rule:
For every node, all values in its left subtree are smaller and all values in its right subtree are larger.

## The Algorithm
The key idea : because of the BST rule, comparing a value with a node tells us which whole subtree to ignore (left if smaller, right if larger). 
So every operation follows a single path from the root downwards.
  FindNode(value)
    1. Start at the root.
    2. If the current node is empty, the value is not in the tree: False.
    3. If the value equals the node's value: True.
    4. If the value is smaller, continue with the left child; otherwise
       continue with the right child. Repeat from step 2.

  AddNode(value)
    1. If the tree is empty, the new node becomes the root.
    2. Otherwise walk down as in FindNode.
    3. If the value equals a node's value, stop (duplicates are ignored).
    4. When the child in the chosen direction is empty, attach the new
       node there. A new node is always a leaf.

  PrintTree()
    1. Print the left subtree (recursively), then the node's value, then
       the right subtree (recursively).
    2. Since left values < node < right values at every node, the values
       come out in sorted order.

  DeleteNode(value)
    1. Find the node as in FindNode. If it does not exist, change nothing.
    2. Leaf (no children): remove it (the parent's link becomes empty).
    3. One child: replace the node by its child.
    4. Two children: find the predecessor (go to the left child, then go
       right as long as possible). Copy its value into the node, then
       delete the predecessor from the left subtree (it has no right
       child, so this is case 2 or 3).
    Why this is correct: the predecessor is larger than every other value
    in the left subtree and smaller than every value in the right
    subtree, so after the copy the BST rule still holds. 
    The method is recursive and returns the new root of each subtree, so
    the parent re-attaches it (node.left = delete(node.left, value)).
    This also covers deleting the root without a special case.

## Code explanation 
Node, BST classes      the Node ADT and BST ADT described above.
findNodeInTree(node, value)    the FindNode steps (recursive).
treeInsert(node, value)        the AddNode steps.
printSubTree(node)      the inorder traversal used by PrintTree.
treeDelete(node, value)  the DeleteNode steps.
getLargest(node)       follows right links to the largest node of a
subtree; used to find the predecessor.
AddNode, FindNode, DeleteNode, PrintTree are the methods of BST that call 
the functions above. AddNode and DeleteNode also update self.root.

## How to run the code
No input is required. The program runs a demonstration (main):
    1. It generates a random number of unique values (between 5 and 50),
       each between 1 and 1000, prints them, and inserts them into an
       empty BST with AddNode.
    2. It prints the tree with PrintTree (sorted order).
    3. It adds a new value that is not already present and prints the tree.
    4. It deletes a randomly chosen existing value and prints the tree.
    5. It searches with FindNode for a value that is in the tree
       (positive test: the value that was added) and for a value that is
       not in the tree (negative test).
  Because the values are random, the output is different on every run.

Problem 2: Counting vowels decoding of Morse Code
## The Algorithm
The table has one row per offset (position in the string) and three
columns for vowels of length 1, 2 and 3 symbols.
Cell [offset][length-1] holds the number of ways to decode everything
BEFORE the offset, followed by a vowel of that length that starts at the
offset. It holds 0 if no vowel of that length starts there (the symbols
do not spell a vowel, or not enough symbols are left).
P(offset) = number of ways to decode the symbols before the offset.
Base case:  P(0) = 1 (one way to decode nothing).
Recurrence: P(offset) = cell[offset-1][length 1]
                          + cell[offset-2][length 2]
                          + cell[offset-3][length 3]
(terms with a negative offset are left out)
This works because the last vowel of any decoding has length 1, 2 or 3
and these three groups do not overlap.
The table is filled from left to right in a single pass, so every cell
that is needed is already filled in.
Answer = P(n), computed with the same diagonal sum one position past the
end of the string.

## Code explanation
vowel_at_position_with_len_1(offset, list)  True if a "." starts at offset (E).
vowel_at_position_with_len_2(offset, list)  True if ".-" or ".." starts at
offset (A or I). It first checks that two symbols are left.
vowel_at_position_with_len_3(offset, list)  True if "---" or "..-"
starts at offset (O or U). It first checks that three symbols are left.
reverse_vowel_at_diagonal_position(offset, matrix)  computes P(offset)
by adding the three diagonal cells described above.
vowel_combinations(list)  fills the table offset by offset and returns
P(n).
main()  reads n and the string, calls vowel_combinations and prints the result.

## How to run the code
From the folder with the files (on Windows, use py instead of python3 if python3 is not recognized):
py vowel_combinations.py < vowel_input1.txt
py vowel_combinations.py < vowel_input2.txt                                
py vowel_combinations.py < vowel_input3.txt
py vowel_combinations.py < vowel_input4.txt

Problem 3: Longest alternating increasing sequence
## The Algorithm
Idea: build the sequence as a chain, and ask one question about each element:
end_A(p) = the length of the longest valid sequence that ends exactly at A[p]
end_B(p) = the same for B[p]  
The element just before A[p] in the chain must come from B, at an earlier position 
q < p, with a smaller value B[q] < A[p]. So:
end_A(p) = 1 + the largest end_B(q) over all q < p with B[q] < A[p]
(or just 1 if no such q exists)
end_B(p) = 1 + the largest end_A(q) over all q < p with A[q] < B[p]
(or just 1 if no such q exists)
The answer is the largest value of end_A(p) or end_B(p) over all positions p.
Memoization: the same question (for example end_A(1)) would be asked many times,
so each answer is stored in a dictionary (memo_A, memo_B) the first time it is
computed and looked up afterwards. Without this the program takes exponential time.

## Code explanation
longest_sequence(A, B)  runs the algorithm and returns the length.
end_A(p), end_B(p)    the two memoized functions described above.
"best = 1"         the element alone is a valid sequence.
"for q in range(p)" tries every earlier position as the previous element.
"if p in memo"     looks up an answer that was already computed.
final loops: take the maximum over all positions of A and B.
main()  asks for the file name, reads the four lines of the file (sizes
on lines 1-2, values on lines 3-4), calls longest_sequence and prints the result.

## How to run the code
py longest_sequence.py
When the program prints "File name:", type the name of the input file,
for example longest_seq1.txt, and press Enter. Example output:
File Input: longest_seq1.txt
Longest Sequence: 4
Input file format (4 lines): size of A, size of B, the values of A
separated by spaces, the values of B separated by spaces.
                
                                
                                
